{
 "result": "Built primes.py: an odd-only bytearray sieve that counts primes strictly below int(argv[1]). It handles n<3 as 0, sizes a bytearray of n>>1 slots where index i represents 2i+1, clears index 0 (the number 1), then crosses out odd multiples of each prime p<=isqrt(n-1) using a strided slice assignment (sieve[start::p] = bytes(count)), which does the clearing in C rather than a Python loop; finally it returns sieve.count(1)+1 to account for the prime 2.\n\nCorrectness verified with the required check and a wider ladder of values: 0->0, 1->0, 2->0, 3->1, 6->3, 10->4, 100->25, 1000->168, 100000->9592, 1000000->78498, 5000000->348513 (matches the specified correct count exactly), 100000000->5761455 (matches pi(1e8)). The registered assay (python3 assay.py, which validates {6,10,100,1000,100000,1000000,5000000} then times 24 subprocess invocations at n=5e6 and n=1e6) passed 5x with a median baseline of ~1535.5 and 2.7% noise, confirming both validity and stable timing. Notes: attempts to go faster via a compiled C extension were blocked by the sandbox (cc cannot write its temp/cache files, /tmp not writable), so the pure-Python odd-only slice sieve is the fastest verified implementation achievable in this environment.",
